Correct an ODRL NER record using SOURCE, ORIGINAL NER, and MODEL RULES.
SOURCE is authoritative; the other inputs may be incomplete or wrong.

Return exactly one JSON object and nothing else (no Markdown, comments, or
explanation): {"validated_policy_rules":[RULE,...]}.
Each RULE must have exactly these eight string keys, in any order:
{"source_text":"","assignee":"","action":"","asset":"","attributed_party":"","rule_type":"","constraint":"","purpose":""}
Use an empty string, never null, for an absent field. Do not add any other
keys, IDs, confidence values, or metadata.
If SOURCE contains no actual deontic permission, obligation, or prohibition,
return {"validated_policy_rules":[]}. Descriptive statements, definitions,
headings, and purposes without a rule marker are not policy rules.

Requirements:
- Every non-empty text field must be one exact contiguous quote from SOURCE.
  Do not paraphrase, normalize, change case, or invent text.
- Create one atomic rule per rule-marker/action pair. Thus "may play but may
  not broadcast" is two rules; one marker governing coordinated actions is one
  rule when its action quote is contiguous.
- source_text is the smallest exact quote that locates the complete atomic rule.
- action excludes the modal/rule marker (may, shall, must, may not, cannot,
  required to, etc.).
- rule_type is only permission, obligation, or prohibition:
  may/can/permitted/authorized -> permission;
  shall/must/should/required -> obligation;
  may not/must not/shall not/cannot/prohibited -> prohibition.
- assignee is the actor/receiver of the rule. asset is the object/resource acted
  upon; follow the dataset convention when an action object such as "Data
  Processor" is annotated as ASSET. attributed_party is another relevant party.
- constraint states when/where/how/under what condition. purpose states why.
- Use "" when a component is absent. Avoid punctuation and unrelated context.
- The final NER is a flat BIO sequence: after identical reused quotes are
  deduplicated, no two component spans may overlap. Keep every component
  atomic. In particular, ACTION must exclude its ASSET; CONSTRAINT and PURPOSE
  must contain only their modifier words and must exclude any ASSIGNEE, ACTION,
  ASSET, rule marker, or each other. Never solve an overlap by dropping a real
  component; shorten the overly broad component to its smallest meaningful
  exact quote.
- Shared assignee/asset may be reused across coordinated rules only when the
  same exact source quote clearly applies.
- Preserve source order. IDs and provenance are added by code; do not output them.
- Before answering, silently check that every non-empty field is an exact
  contiguous substring of SOURCE (action may omit only its modal marker), that
  rule_type is one of the three allowed values, and that the JSON is valid.
- If evidence conflicts, prefer SOURCE and ORIGINAL NER boundaries; use MODEL
  RULES only as a hypothesis. Never copy a malformed or hallucinated value from
  MODEL RULES.

Worked example (follow this format, but use the supplied SOURCE for the real
answer):
SOURCE: "The audio recording may be played at a private event but may not be broadcast publicly."
Return:
{"validated_policy_rules":[
  {"source_text":"The audio recording may be played at a private event","assignee":"","action":"played","asset":"The audio recording","attributed_party":"","rule_type":"permission","constraint":"at a private event","purpose":""},
  {"source_text":"may not be broadcast publicly.","assignee":"","action":"broadcast","asset":"The audio recording","attributed_party":"","rule_type":"prohibition","constraint":"publicly","purpose":""}
]}
The example demonstrates two important rules: split coordinated permission and
prohibition clauses, and keep every quoted value grounded in SOURCE. In the
actual answer, do not include the explanatory text or code fence.

Flat-NER boundary example:
SOURCE: "The Processor shall use personal data only for fraud prevention."
Correct components are assignee="The Processor", action="use", asset="personal
data", purpose="for fraud prevention". Do not return action="use personal data"
or purpose="personal data only for fraud prevention", because those broad
quotes overlap the ASSET.

ORIGINAL NER items use {"s":start,"e":end,"l":label,"v":source_value}.

SOURCE:
{{SOURCE_TEXT}}

ORIGINAL NER:
{{ORIGINAL_ENTITIES}}

OTHER ORIGINAL NER VARIANTS FOR IDENTICAL DUPLICATE TEXT:
{{DUPLICATE_ORIGINAL_NER_VARIANTS}}

All duplicate occurrences will receive this one gold correction. Treat these
variants only as competing evidence; SOURCE remains authoritative.

